:feedback
!ifval $val62+$val66=0 and $val58 isitemof $(val51[2,3]),$(val52[2,3])
 <div class='oef_feedbacks'>	 Disordered logic:	 how did you manage to compute  
!insmath f'(x)
$() before you had even defined	 
!insmath f
$()&nbsp;?	
$()</div>
!endif
!ifval $val66*($val65+$val69)=1
 <div class='oef_feedbacks'>	 Error&nbsp;: to get 
!insmath f($val20) = a
$(), you have to take	 
!insmath f(x) = $val30
$(), instead of 
!insmath f(x) = $val33
$().	
$()</div>
!endif
!ifval $val62*$val67=1 or $val66*$val63=1
 <div class='oef_feedbacks'>	 Error! Did you calculate the derivative of 
!insmath f
$()&nbsp;?	
$()</div>
!endif
!ifval $val62+$val66=1 and $val63+$val67=0
 <div class='oef_feedbacks'>	 Your second step is premature.	
$()</div>
!endif
!ifval $val63*$val68=1
 <div class='oef_feedbacks'>	 You made a mistake in calculating the sign of 
!insmath f'(x)
$(). Check it out.	 
$()</div>
!endif
!ifval $val64*$val69=1 or $val68*$val65=1
 <div class='oef_feedbacks'>	 You misunderstood the sign in the last step. Think about it:	 if 
!insmath f'(x) $val37 0
$() for 
!insmath x \in [a,b]
$(), is  
!insmath f
$() increasing or	 decreasing? So do we have 
!insmath f(a) $val37 f(b)
$()	 or 
!insmath f(b) $val37 f(a)
$()?	
$()</div>
!endif
